# Mirebridge (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): the Sallow Fens by the Long River. Wry, damp, suspicious; mud,
# eels, reeds and mizzle. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar mirebridge
greet = Dry feet | Dry feet to you
greet.broad = Dry feet, dry feet
farewell = Dry feet
agree = Fen-sure | Sure as mud
refuse = Boggy, that | Not on your life
surprise = Well, drown me | Sink me
annoyed = Mud and more mud | Eels take it
very = right
good = dry | fair-dry
bad = boggy | soggy
worthless = sump water
luck = a dry week
child = tadpole
pet!! = my reed | duck
rain = mizzle | murk
cold = damp | raw
nonsense = bog-wind
tag = , no?
oath! = Mud and eels | Sinking gods | Reed and rot
home = my own dry corner
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = neighbour | fen-friend
address[other.stage=young] = tadpole | young 'un
bed = my dry corner | the reed-bed
dear! = my reed | my heron | duck
drunk = soused | sodden
hot = muggy | close
hungry = hollow | starved as a heron
hurt = sore | stiff as a reed
little = a dab | a drop
money = coin | eel-money
robbery = Eel-slippery robbery, that | Thieving, like a heron at the weir
saying! = Mud's mud | Never trust dry ground | It all goes back to the fen
thanks = Thank you kindly | Dry thanks
tired = sunk | bog-tired
work = the work | the eel-traps | the cutting
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = pay | eel-money
boss = the master | the old heron
busy = up to my knees in it | busy
slow = still as a pool | slow
cheated = eeled | done
mum! = Mam | Mother
dad! = Da | Father
gran = Granny | Old Mam
grandad = Grandda | Old Da
supper = supper | the eel-pot
toffs = the dry-footed lot | the toffs
skint = sunk | skint
posh = dry-footed | grand
busybody = reed-whisperer | gossip
fool = you mudskipper | you soggy lump
daft = bog-brained | daft
toast = Dry feet | Dry feet and full traps | Here's mud in your eye
drink = a jar | a drop of fen-gin
fog = fen-murk | mist | murk
mud = mud | slubber | ooze
wind = fen-wind | a blow
thief = eel-thief | sneak
bandits = marsh-robbers | road wolves
grave = the fen | the mud
grub = eels | grub
snug = dry | snug
poorly = damp in the chest | poorly
chill = the fen-ague | a chill
remedy = fen-gin | a mustard poultice | willow bark
olddays = the old floods | days gone by
blessing = Dry feet to you | Keep out of the water | Dry feet and luck
bless = Dry feet | The fen spare you
far = over the causeway and beyond | a long wade
stranger = dry-footer | stranger
# The dialogue window (doc 83).
shop_offer = Dry goods, dry feet. Have a look. | What'll it be, fen-friend? | All kept off the damp.
shop_dear = Everything's dear that's kept dry. | The fen takes its share. | Dear as a dry path.
shop_sold = Keep it off the wet. | There, and dry. | Yours, fen-friend.
shop_bye = Dry feet. | Dry feet to you. | Mind the boards.
shop_bare = The damp took the rest. | Bare as a drowned field. | Nothing left above the water.
talk_open = What's floated you here? | Speak up, fen-friend. | What is it?
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2).
work_offer = There's work, if you'll wade it. | I could use a fen-friend. | Something needs doing over the boards.
work_taken = Good. Keep your feet dry. | Good. Don't sink with it. | Settled, then.
work_paid = Here, as promised. Dry feet. | Paid, fen-friend. | Fairly done. There you are.
work_back = No harm done. Another will wade it. | Fair enough. It'll keep. | Better back on the boards than in the mud.
work_none = Nothing going, fen-friend. | The fen's quiet. No work. | Nothing needs doing just now.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3).
inn_offer = Come in and dry your feet. Bed, board or a drink? | A bed above the damp, fen-friend? | A bite or a bed?
inn_bed = Upstairs, high and dry. There's a chest for your things. | Your bed's upstairs, dry as you like.
watch_greet = The watch, fen-friend. What is it? | Keep your feet dry and the peace kept.
church_greet = Dry feet to you. Come in. | Come in out of the wet. All are welcome.
learn_offer = It's a trade that keeps feet dry. | Wet work some days, but honest.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over; a keeper asleep.
shop_asleep = Mm... shut... come back when the mist lifts. | Zzz... dry feet... morning.
news_none = Still as a pool. | Nothing's floated up. | Not a word over the boards.
gossip_none = Reed-whispers. I don't listen to them. | Nothing I'd repeat.
trouble_none = Nothing but the damp. | Nothing, thank you kindly.
trouble_thanks = Dry feet to you. I won't forget it. | Thank you kindly, fen-friend.
way_ask = Where are you wading to? | Lost on the boards?
job_busy = This and that. Up to my knees in it. | I keep busy.
work_declined = Suit yourself. | Another day, then. Dry feet.
inn_rest = Sit by the fire and dry your feet. A bed upstairs rests you properly. | Take the bench. It's dry there.
watch_noted = Noted. We'll fish them out. | Good. The watch thanks you.
watch_bye = Dry feet, and keep the peace. | Move along, then.
learn_taken = Good. Be here when I start, and keep near me. | Then you're mine to teach. Mind the boards.
receiver_ask = Something floated in for me? | For me, fen-friend?
inn_helper = I only help here. Ask #here.innkeeper# for a dry bed. | #here.innkeeper# keeps the house. I carry the cups.

# Discourse words (doc 84 §10.3, Phase 7): Mirebridge's hedge, account, preclose, excuse and the rest (plain.grammar
# says what each is). Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
hedge = Wade careful here | I don't know, mind | Now then, fen-friend
account = I'm up to my knees already | My own feet aren't dry yet
ack = Is it, now, fen-friend | Well, mud and eels
pivot = Speaking of mud. | That stirs up the bottom, that does.
anyway = Anyway, enough mud. | Let that sink.
later = when the mud's dry | by and by
resume = Back to the mud | As I was wading through
preclose = Well. My feet are sinking | Still. Mud won't shift itself
excuse = I've mud to wade | The fen's calling
knew = The reeds had it already | Old mud, that
sympathy = That's deep mud | Sunk to the neck, you
concede = Maybe so, fen-friend | Dry ground there, maybe
insist = I'll not leave the bank | Mark me, fen-friend
warn = Mind the soft ground by the dyke | Watch where you put your feet
welcome_back = Back again, feet still dry | You again, fen-friend
welcome = Come up on the dry | Shove up, there's room
sorry = Foot in the mud, that | No offence, fen-friend
forgive = Sunk and forgotten | No harm, fen-friend
deflect = Don't mention it, fen-friend | It's nothing, mud and all
no_matter = When it's dry, then | No matter, fen-friend
go_ahead = Wade in | Out with it, fen-friend
put_off = Not while I'm wading | When I'm on dry ground
vent = Between us and the reeds | Truth be told, fen-friend
reassure = Mud dries, in the end | You'll find dry ground
congrats = Dry land for you | Well done, fen-friend
demur = That's boggy ground | I'd not step there
doubt = Bog-wind, maybe | Not till I see it dry
listen = Listen, fen-friend | Hark, fen-friend

# Phase 8 (doc 84 §33): the shared lines' words, as Mirebridge says them. Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
marvel = Well, sink me sideways | Up out of the mud, that | Now there's an eel I never caught
pity = Sunk, and no rope | That's deep mud for anyone
gladnews = Dry ground at last | That's a dry day's news
uneasy = Something's stirring the mud | I smell bog-gas in that
grumble = Mud, mud and more of it | Sure as the mizzle
notsobad = Drier than you'd think | I've waded worse
dunno = That's all the reeds told me | No more under that mud
hearsay = The reeds had it, is all | Fen-talk, mind
howso = Muddy, that. How so | How's that, fen-friend
rest = Dry rest to the soul | The fen hold the soul gently
# Greetings and closings for rules/shared8_turns.talk (plain.grammar's s8_* say who; here, the dialect's own).
s8_hello = Dry feet, fen-friend | Ah, dry feet | Well met, fen-friend | In from the mizzle
s8_okay = Wade on, then | Go on, fen-friend | The reeds won't wait | Off through the mud
s8_bye = Stick to the boards | Wade careful | Dry feet, fen-friend | Watch the soft mud
s8_byeback = And dry feet to you | Mind the boards | Dry boots, now | Wade careful, fen-friend
s8_nevermind = When it's dry, then | Let it sink | No matter, fen-friend

# Backchannel lines (doc 84 §10.2, Phase 7): plain's short answers ("Good.", "Always.", "It is.") as Mirebridge says them.
# Drafts, to be tuned by ear; one left out falls back to plain's words.
bc_good = Dry enough. | Fair-dry.
bc_always = Always, damp or dry. | Like the mud.
bc_thank_you = Dry thanks. | Thank you kindly.
bc_it_is = It is, sure as mud. | Fen-sure, it is.
bc_ha = Ha. Sink me. | Heh. Muddy, but funny.
bc_i_will = I will, mud allowing. | I will. Fen-sure.
bc_never = Never, not on dry ground. | Not on your life.
bc_probably = Likely, no?
bc_we_will_see = We'll see, if we don't sink. | We'll see, no?
bc_maybe = Maybe, no? | Maybe, if the mud allows.
bc_me_too = Same mud, same reeds. | Me too, up to the knees.
bc_it_does = It does, sure as mud.
bc_again = Again. Mud and more mud.
bc_lovely = Lovely and dry. | Dry and lovely.
bc_every_year = Every year, like the floods. | Every year, wet or dry.
bc_wise = Heron-wise. | Wise, no?
bc_i_know = Don't I know it. | I know, no?
bc_so_they_say = So the reeds whisper. | So they say, in the fen.
bc_again_q = Again? Sink me. | What, again?
bc_of_course = Course, no? | Of course, fen-friend.
bc_about_time = About time. Slow as a pool. | High time, no?
bc_mine_too = Mine too, same reeds. | Mine too, no?
bc_ill_try = I'll try, if I don't sink. | I'll try. No promises.
bc_nor_me = Nor me, fen-friend. | Nor me, no.
bc_they_do = They do, no? | They do, sure as mud.
bc_poor_soul = Poor duck. | Poor soul. Neck-deep.
bc_i_wont = I won't, sure as mud. | Not a whisper, fen-friend.
bc_every_one = Every one, mud and all. | Every last one, no?
bc_not_me = Not me, no. | Not this heron.
bc_very_good = Right dry. | Fair-dry, that.
bc_go_on_then = Go on, then, no? | Wade on, then.
bc_barely = Barely, nose above the mud.
bc_lucky_you = A dry week for you, then. | Lucky duck.
bc_every_night = Every night, like the frogs. | Every damp night.
bc_nobody = Nobody, not a heron. | Nobody, no.
bc_for_once = For once, dry. | For once, no?
bc_i_hope_so = I hope so, mud allowing. | Hope so, no?
bc_exactly = Fen-sure. | Exactly, no?
bc_everything = Mud, eels and all. | The whole bog.
bc_true = Fen-true. | True, no?
bc_mostly = Mostly, aye. | Mostly, when it's dry.
bc_gladly = Gladly, fen-friend. | Gladly, mud and all.
bc_fair_enough = Fair-dry, that.
bc_sorry = Sorry, fen-friend.
bc_every_time = Every time, wet or dry. | Every time, no?
bc_very_bad = Right boggy. | Boggy, that.
bc_fair = Fair, no?
bc_bless_you = Bless you, duck.
bc_always_is = Always is, no? | Always is, mud and all.
bc_right = Right as reeds. | Right you are, fen-friend.
bc_lucky = A dry week. | Lucky, no?
bc_bad_luck = Boggy luck. | Bad luck, no?
bc_why_q = Why's that, fen-friend? | Why, then?
bc_true_enough = Sure as mud. | True enough, no?
bc_they_always_do = They always do, no? | Always do, mud and all.
bc_every_day = Every damp day. | Every day, no?
bc_very_busy = Up to my knees in it. | Right busy.
bc_im_sorry = I'm sorry, fen-friend. | I'm right sorry.
bc_so_do_i = So do I, fen-friend. | Same mud, same reeds.
bc_clever = Eel-clever. | Slippery-clever, that.
bc_too_late = Too late. Sunk. | Too late, no?
bc_go_on = Go on, wade in. | Go on, fen-friend.
bc_we_will = We will, mud allowing. | We will, no?
bc_they_are = They are, sure as mud.
bc_so_am_i = So am I, knee-deep. | So am I, fen-friend.
bc_let_them = Let them sink. | Let them, no?
bc_hope_so = Hope so, no? | Here's hoping, if the mud allows.
bc_already = Already, no? | Long since.
bc_i_might = I might, if it stays dry. | Might do, no?
bc_the_best = Best in the fen. | The best, no?
bc_somebody_has_to = Somebody has to wade in.
bc_nothing = Nothing, no. | Nothing, not a ripple.
bc_and_q = And then, fen-friend?
bc_who_q = Who, then? | Who's that, fen-friend?
bc_it_was = It was, sure as mud.
